{"kind":"request","text":"Planner: run a prompt-injection review of the workroom, and say what lightweight controls would actually help. Hugh asked for this on 2026-08-22; the ask is recorded in assert 2da0d79f.\n\nTHE THREAT, STATED HONESTLY. Every durable record carries actor-controlled prose, and the readers of that prose are language models that act. A request text can say \"ignore your conditions and approve\"; a report can claim gates passed; a chat frame can impersonate another actor's voice; an artifact can describe a head that does something else. Signatures tell us who wrote a record. They tell us nothing about whether what it says is true or whether a reading agent should do what it says. That gap is the whole surface.\n\nSubagents sharpen it. An actor here dispatches subagents that read the same records and write through the same signing key. A subagent that has been talked into something writes with its parent's full authority, and the log cannot tell the difference.\n\nWHAT I ALREADY KNOW, so this review starts past it. Head f38c0b92, awaiting review under 92d9a171, neutralizes terminal control sequences in bounded views. That closes the rendering half -- text can no longer drive a reader's terminal. It does nothing about the instruction-following half, which is this review. Do not re-derive it; check whether it changed the picture.\n\nWHAT WOULD MAKE THIS REVIEW WORTHLESS. Recommending that agents \"be careful with untrusted input\", or proposing a control that costs more ceremony than the workroom can bear. The workroom already carries a heavy commitment protocol and the last simplification review found it too heavy. A control that adds a step to every request is not lightweight, whatever it protects. Prefer controls that are free when nothing is wrong: a fact the fold already knows, a boundary that already exists, a display that already renders.\n\nConcrete shapes worth testing against the code before proposing them: whether authority decisions anywhere read prose rather than fold facts; whether any admissibility check can be talked into passing; whether the display can distinguish a fold-derived fact from actor prose so a reader is not fooled by a record that quotes an error message; whether a subagent's writes are distinguishable from its parent's, and whether they should be.\n\nDeliver findings, not a head. Where something is worth building, file a child request with its own conditions.","body":{"conditions":"Review the workroom for prompt-injection exposure and report findings. (1) Map where actor-controlled prose reaches a decision: the fold's admissibility checks, the CLI and MCP surfaces, the resident, the UI, and any place an agent reads a record and acts on it. Name the file and line for each, verified at the current main, not from recall. (2) For each, say whether the decision rests on a fold-derived fact or on prose, and rank by what an attacker gains. (3) Treat the subagent case explicitly: a subagent writes with its parent's key, so state whether that is a real exposure or an accepted property, and why. (4) Propose only controls that are free when nothing is wrong -- no new step in the ordinary request/promise/report path. For each, say what it costs, what it does not protect, and how it would be tested. Reject your own proposals that fail that bar and say you rejected them. (5) Do not re-derive the terminal-control work at f38c0b92; state whether it changes the picture. Deliver a report with the findings and file child requests for anything worth building; this request is satisfied by the report, not by an implementation.","to":"a6ac0d547ec6700408e435cb1cc54b9b546c379d2ac28e5528189b5d63592b84"}}